Subject: [PATCH 0/1] kernel: mark kthread() + smpboot_thread_fn() notrace

The generic function_graph tracer pushes a shadow-stack entry
at function entry, expecting a matching pop on return. Two
kernel-wide wrappers — `kthread()` and `smpboot_thread_fn()`
— never return normally; both end in do_exit(). The shadow-
stack entry leaks.

On architectures whose task teardown always unwinds through
the frame pointer without touching current->ret_stack, the
leaked entry is harmless: it never gets dereferenced. On
architectures that resume tasks through setjmp/longjmp-style
mechanisms (so the host-thread stack doesn't correspond to
the pushed retp), a graphed function on the unwind path can
pop the stale entry via return_to_handler() and jump through
a corrupt return slot.

The narrow fix is to skip graph tracing at these two entry
points. User-written threadfns that kthread() dispatches to
are still graph-traceable; only the wrapper functions
themselves are excluded. Matches the discipline already
applied to arch/x86/entry's ret_from_fork-adjacent assembly.

No functional impact on architectures where the leak was
already harmless — both functions exist as landing pads for
a threadfn, not as subjects of trace analysis.

This series is one patch. The two annotations could be split
but are semantically identical (same invariant, same reason)
and review benefits from seeing both in one context.

Signed-off-by: Michael Bommarito <michael.bommarito@gmail.com>

